# Shared-trade Universal Links (see ddbx-ios-app investigations/2026-06-07-share-trade-deep-link.md).
# When the app is installed the OS intercepts these before the request reaches
# the network (iOS via apple-app-site-association, Android via assetlinks.json)
# and opens the app. For people WITHOUT the app, Pages Functions now own these
# routes and user-agent-sniff iOS vs Android:
#   - /t/{id}    → functions/t/[id].js     (UK conversion funnel; Android → Play)
#   - /us/t/{id} → functions/us/t/[id].js  (US store redirect; Android → Play)
# They take precedence over _redirects, so no static store rule lives here.

# /api -> /developers is NOT here: Pages runs Functions before static asset
# serving, and functions/_middleware.js matches every route, so a rule for /api
# never fired (it answered 200 in production). That redirect lives in
# functions/api.js instead. See the note there.
#
# /developers is canonical because /api is the same-origin API prefix — the
# vite dev proxy forwards /api/* to wrangler, and keeping the page there would
# foreclose ever proxying the worker under this origin. The nav still reads
# "API"; the label and the path don't have to match.
/developers/*      /developers                               301!

# SPA fallback — MUST stay last. The site previously relied on Cloudflare's
# implicit single-page-app fallback; introducing this _redirects file makes it
# explicit so client routes (/dealings/:id, /directors/:id, …) keep resolving.
# Static assets (incl. /.well-known/apple-app-site-association) are served before
# these rules, so the AASA file is returned as JSON, not swallowed by this line.
/*         /index.html                               200
